Here's a concise comparison of **Iterative vs Recursive** approaches:

| Feature              | Iterative                           | Recursive                                 |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| **Definition**       | Uses loops (for/while) repeatedly   | Function calls itself                     |
| **Memory Usage**     | Constant (uses loop variables only) | Higher (each call adds a new stack frame) |
| **Performance**      | Often faster due to less overhead   | Can be slower due to call stack           |
| **Readability**      | Simple for straightforward logic    | More elegant for problems like trees      |
| **Risk**             | Less prone to stack overflow        | Risk of stack overflow if no base case    |
| **Example Problems** | Loops, counters, accumulations      | Tree/graph traversal, divide & conquer    |

---

### Example: Fibonacci

**Iterative:**

```ruby
def fib_iter(n)
  a, b = 0, 1
  n.times { a, b = b, a + b }
  a
end
```

**Recursive:**

```ruby
def fib_rec(n)
  return n if n <= 1
  fib_rec(n - 1) + fib_rec(n - 2)
end
```

